c{3k}{2} \\), \\( c = c \\), and \\( d = k \\) into the discriminant:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 18a \\cdot \\frac{3k}{2} \\cdot kc - 4\\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right)^3 k + \\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right)^2 c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2 k^2.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the discriminant:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 81ak^2 c - \\frac{27k^3}{4} + \\frac{9k^2 c^2}{4} - 4ac^3 - 27a^2 k^2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\nStep 7: Find the condition for three distinct real roots.\nFor the polynomial to have three distinct real roots, \\( \\Delta > 0 \\). Since \\( \\Delta \\) is a quadratic in \\( a \\) (and other terms), the condition is satisfied for \\( a > 0 \\). Therefore, the largest value of \\( k \\) occurs when \\( a = 0 \\). However, \\( a \\) cannot be zero because the polynomial would degenerate to a quadratic.\n\n---\n\nStep 8: Re-evaluate the discriminant for \\( a = 0 \\).\nSubstitute \\( a = 0 \\) into the discriminant:\n\\[\n\\Delta = -\\frac{27k^3}{4} + \\frac{9k^2 c^2}{4} - 4c^3 - 27k^2.\n\\]\n\nFor three distinct real roots, \\( \\Delta > 0 \\). The largest \\( k \\) occurs when \\( c \\) is minimized. The smallest \\( c \\) can be is zero. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\Delta = \\frac{27k^3}{4} - 27k^2 > 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 27k^2(k - 4) > 0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k > 0 \\), the largest \\( k \\) is \\( k = 4 \\).\n\n---\n\nFinal Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
